/* Pass 15: canonical chapter-complete voice corrections for nine existing chapter slots. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-21":{"number":21,"title":"The Guest Who Stayed","content":"Greg arrived at Tideglass Cove with two broken folding chairs and an unfinished song.\n\nI knew the song was unfinished because he'd hummed the same four notes through the Starwake Door, across the square, and down the steps to the beach without finding a fifth.\n\nMaui heard him before I did.\n\n\"Is that music,\" he asked, \"or is someone being chased by a very polite goat?\"\n\nGreg looked offended for a second.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\n\"Little of both.\"\n\nHe set the chairs beside the workshop. One had a bent hinge. The other wobbled because a screw had stripped the wood beneath its bracket.\n\n\"These are for the rest bench by the sea-glass orchard,\" he explained. \"Pip said the older ones weren't comfortable for people waiting for the ferry.\"\n\nMaui lifted the wobbly chair.\n\n\"I can fix that. Well, I can lift it until the hinge becomes somebody else's problem. The lifting is definitely the impressive part.\"\n\nGreg reached for the other.\n\n\"Hey, I've got tools.\"\n\nMaui flexed his shoulders.\n\n\"I can carry both.\"\n\n\"Great, but can you fix both?\"\n\n\"I can do anything.\"\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nHere we went.\n\nThey were both trying to be kind. That was the problem. Maui wanted to be the helpful hero; Greg wanted to be the man who showed up when a job needed doing. Neither had asked the person who'd requested the chairs what the job actually involved.\n\nPip arrived carrying a folded sketch.\n\n\"Good, you're here,\" they said.\n\n\"We've got it,\" Maui announced.\n\n\"Do you?\" Pip asked.\n\nGreg held up a screwdriver.\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nPip unfolded the sketch.\n\nThe bench needed two chairs with stable seats and backs at slightly different heights. One person using the sheltered garden found it difficult to stand from the low seat. Another wanted armrests they could grip. The chairs also had to fit on the path without blocking anyone using wheels or a handcart.\n\nNeither broken chair was the right size.\n\nMaui looked at the drawing.\n\nGreg looked at the chairs.\n\nI bit the inside of my cheek to stop smiling.\n\n\"Maybe we should ask the person who needs them,\" I said.\n\nPip nodded.\n\n\"That was my next sentence.\"\n\nWe found Ilya, the garden steward's assistant, checking the first sea-kale bed. They had been the one to request seating because they spent long stretches weeding and sometimes needed to rest before walking back uphill.\n\n\"These are too low,\" Ilya said when we showed the chairs.\n\nGreg examined the legs.\n\n\"I could add blocks.\"\n\n\"That might make them unstable,\" Ilya said. \"And the path is narrow here.\"\n\nMaui put the chairs down.\n\n\"What would help?\"\n\nIlya looked surprised.\n\n\"A bench with a firm seat and two armrests. Nothing fancy. I want to sit without sinking into it, and I want to be able to stand without somebody having to pull me up.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\n\"Then let's build that.\"\n\nPip smiled.\n\n\"Now we're talking.\"\n\nThe afternoon became a lesson in designing for the person who would use the thing, rather than the person who wanted the satisfaction of finishing it.\n\nTamsin provided measurements. Edda checked the turning space. Ilya tested a temporary model made from a crate and two spare boards. Greg sanded the edges. Maui held the supports while the screws went in.\n\nI watched them work, amused by how quickly the competition dissolved once there was an actual specification.\n\nThen Greg started humming again.\n\nFour notes. A pause. Four notes.\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"What's that song about?\"\n\nGreg shrugged.\n\n\"Coming back to a place that's changed.\"\n\n\"Sounds sad.\"\n\n\"Some of it is.\"\n\n\"Make it heroic.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\n\"That's one option.\"\n\nMaui began proposing lyrics involving storms, monsters, and the triumph of returning across a thousand seas. Greg tried the rhythm out of politeness. It was terrible.\n\nGreg stopped picking at the guitar strings.\n\n\"Hey, can I say something without it turning into an epic battle?\"\n\n\"Depends,\" Maui said. \"Is there a monster?\"\n\n\"No monster. When Steven was little, I'd tell myself I'd figure things out before the next song. Turns out there isn't a next-song version where I magically know what I'm doing.\" Greg played the four notes again, leaving the fifth empty. \"Sometimes somebody comes home. Sometimes they don't. I need the song to leave room for both.\"\n\nMaui's face twisted in concentration. \"If I wrote it, there'd be a thousand canoes and an entire island chanting.\"\n\n\"Yeah, man. That's your song.\"\n\nNobody had said Maui's story was worthless. Greg simply wasn't offering him the steering wheel.\n\nFrom the bench, Ilya tapped the armrest. A screw had begun to squeak.\n\n\"Before either of you gets famous, could somebody fetch Tamsin? This needs checking.\"\n\nGreg set his guitar out of the walkway. Maui flourished his arm toward the workshop.\n\n\"At last! A quest with an actual expert!\"\n\n\"Don't carry the bench away,\" I said.\n\n\"Everyone's a critic.\" He grinned and went to fetch her.\n\nGreg waited until Maui had gone before trying the phrase again. The silence in the fifth note didn't feel empty anymore. It felt like a place somebody might choose to enter.\n\n\"Wait,\" Maui announced, \"I haven't reached the part where the monster begs the great Maui for mercy.\"\n\nGreg rested his chin on his guitar. \"Yeah, that's probably gonna be a different song.\"\n\n\"Different? It's an instant classic!\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest and began conducting the imaginary orchestra. He stopped when Greg plucked a soft, uncertain chord and looked thoughtful.\n\n\"What's that sound?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"The one I keep trying to write.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a boat scraping into a harbor after a bad day.\" Maui's grin turned almost shy. \"Not the part where everybody cheers. The part where the people you were worried about are actually there.\"\n\nGreg played the chord again. \"That's... kinda what I meant.\"\n\nMaui sat up, delighted. \"Ha! Brilliant. I contributed! Put that in the credits.\"\n\n\"You contributed a description,\" I said.\n\n\"A legendary description.\"\n\nGreg laughed and kept the next chord for himself. Maui was already composing a speech about being a gifted musician, but for once he waited until Greg finished the measure before giving it.\n\nI told them so.\n\n\"Both of you are making the song about the loudest person in the room,\" I said.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Who should it be about?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It's your song.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms.\n\n\"You said it was terrible.\"\n\n\"It was. I didn't say you have to let me write it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nThey sat beside the unfinished bench while Ilya tested the armrest. For a few minutes nobody spoke.\n\nThen Greg played the four notes again, softer.\n\nIlya paused.\n\n\"That sounds like the ferry bell,\" they said.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\n\"The what?\"\n\n\"The little bell Fen rings when the tide turns and the upper path is open. It's not exciting. It just tells people when to come back.\"\n\nGreg stared at the water.\n\nThen he played a fifth note.\n\nIt fit.\n\nMaui looked at Ilya.\n\n\"That's what the song needed?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nWe finished the bench before sunset. Ilya sat on it, stood without help, and sat again. Edda checked the path clearance and approved the location. The old chairs went back to Starwake for salvageable parts instead of being placed where they would be inconvenient.\n\nPip wrote a maintenance note for the next month's inspection.\n\nMaui looked at the finished bench.\n\n\"No plaque?\"\n\n\"No plaque,\" I said.\n\n\"Not even a small one?\"\n\nPip handed him a pencil.\n\n\"You may write the inspection date underneath.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nGreg stood beside the bench with his guitar.\n\n\"Can I play the song here when it's done?\"\n\nIlya considered the question.\n\n\"As long as you ask before you turn the orchard into a concert.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nThe evening air was cool. The tide had come in enough to cover the old stones where the second bell had sounded. The temporary closure sign remained in place. Someone had drawn a tiny crab in the corner.\n\nMaui slipped an arm around my shoulders.\n\n\"Greg's not bad,\" he said.\n\n\"He's not.\"\n\n\"Bit slow with the monsters.\"\n\n\"Not every song needs one.\"\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\nGreg joined us on the path and looked between us.\n\n\"You two have any plans for dinner?\"\n\n\"I was going to ask Jasper if they'd like to come home,\" Maui said.\n\nGreg looked at me rather than assuming an answer.\n\n\"I was going to ask if you'd be free tomorrow to hear the new chorus. No pressure.\"\n\nI appreciated the difference. The relationships in my life did not have to turn every shared evening into a contest or a secret. Maui knew Greg and I cared about each other. Greg knew Maui mattered to me. Neither of them had to become anything more to the other for that to be true.\n\n\"Tomorrow afternoon works,\" I said. \"Tonight I'm going home with Maui.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Great. I'll bring the fifth note.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Make it a heroic one.\"\n\n\"Maybe a modestly heroic one.\"\n\nThey laughed.\n\nOn the way home, Maui was quiet for a while.\n\n\"Did I make that awkward?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"You were trying to help. So was Greg. Ilya needed both of you to listen first.\"\n\nMaui thought about the bench.\n\n\"I think I'm getting better.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nHe squeezed my hand.\n\n\"Does that make me less impressive?\"\n\nI stopped, turned toward him, and kissed his cheek.\n\n\"No. It makes you easier to love.\"\n\nHe blushed.\n\nThe great Maui, who had bragged about lassoing the sun, blushed beside a public bench.\n\nI decided not to mention it.\n\nAt home, he carved a second tiny fish into the broom handle. This one looked suspiciously like Greg's hair.\n\nI made him promise not to give it to Greg without asking.\n\nHe promised.\n\nMostly.","word_count":1639},"maui-25":{"number":25,"title":"Mereana Returns","content":"Mereana's boat arrived without a banner.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed. The second was that she stepped onto the dock carrying a toolbox, not an instrument.\n\nMaui had been expecting a performance. He'd told three different people that the visiting artists from Aotearoa might be bringing a celebration to the cove, and by noon half the village was hovering near the landing.\n\nMereana looked at the crowd and raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Did someone announce a festival?\"\n\nMaui looked at me. The grin he normally wore when a crowd gathered had frozen halfway across his face. \"Technically,\" he muttered, \"I may have advertised a magnificent artistic occasion before asking the artists whether they wanted one. A minor oversight. Possibly a large oversight.\"\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\n\"Apparently,\" I said, \"someone did.\"\n\nHer group had come to inspect a set of carved panels they had lent to the local heritage workshop. They were returning after an exhibition elsewhere, and Mereana had scheduled a short visit because she wanted to see how the panels were being stored and whether the agreement still suited her group.\n\nIt was their work. They could choose when to display it, discuss it, or take it home.\n\nPip hurried down the steps carrying a clipboard.\n\n\"We prepared the hall,\" they said. \"We can set up chairs if you'd like to perform.\"\n\n\"That's kind,\" Mereana answered. \"But we didn't come to perform.\"\n\nThe disappointment moved through the crowd like a breeze.\n\nA visitor standing near the rope store spoke before I could.\n\n\"But aren't you here to do the traditional courtship dance? Someone told me your group teaches the old way people choose partners.\"\n\nMereana went still.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nThe visitor blinked.\n\n\"I read somewhere—\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"I don't remember.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps don't explain my people to me from a thing you can't remember.\"\n\nHer voice wasn't raised. She didn't need it to be.\n\nI felt my face grow hot on her behalf, which was a familiar and unhelpful sensation. I wanted to step in, make a speech about respect, and rescue everyone from the silence.\n\nMereana was perfectly capable of responding herself.\n\nI stayed beside the supply cart.\n\nThe visitor looked uncomfortable.\n\n\"I didn't mean offense.\"\n\n\"Then you can ask about what we're actually doing,\" Mereana said. \"We are artists. Some of our work draws on specific traditions with specific histories. Those aren't interchangeable with a universal romance ritual, and they aren't yours to assign us because you've come expecting entertainment.\"\n\nPip lowered the clipboard.\n\n\"Would you prefer we close the hall to the public?\"\n\n\"Not necessarily. I'd like to inspect the panels. Then we'll decide whether there's anything we want to share.\"\n\nMaui stepped forward.\n\nMini Maui popped up on his shoulder and raised both arms for the applause that wasn't coming. Maui gave him a long, injured stare.\n\n\"I may have been a tiny bit enthusiastic,\" he admitted.\n\nMereana folded her arms. \"You told half the cove we were doing a performance.\"\n\n\"That sounds worse when you say it.\"\n\n\"It should.\"\n\nHe glanced at me as if I might help him charm his way free. I raised my eyebrows. He sighed with the sort of theatrical suffering normally reserved for a cursed demigod.\n\n\"Okay. Here's what happened. I got excited, pictured a brilliant show, and forgot to ask the people who'd actually have to do it. Big mistake. Very on-brand mistake. Still mine.\"\n\nMereana studied him for a moment. \"We came to inspect the lending rack, Maui. The work is ours to plan.\"\n\n\"Then the famous Maui will provide only the services requested.\" He drew himself upright. \"Heavy objects moved. Doors held. Extraordinary smiling.\"\n\n\"Start with the doors,\" she said.\n\nHe bowed extravagantly. The apology was real, but so was his need to make somebody smile. Mereana didn't owe him a performance for trying.\n\n\"I told people you might perform. I shouldn't have.\"\n\nMereana looked at him.\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'll correct it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat could have been the end of the matter, except the panels had been moved.\n\nNot stolen. Not damaged deliberately. Someone had relocated them to a storage room where the air was drier, without telling Mereana's group. The new room was locked, and the person with the key was away at the market.\n\nMereana looked at the empty display rack.\n\n\"That wasn't our agreement.\"\n\nNeri arrived with the lending record and apologized. There was no excuse to invent. The workshop had made a decision about someone else's work without consulting its owners.\n\nWe found the keyholder. We inspected the storage room. Two panels were fine, one had warped slightly against a support that put pressure on its edge, and the fourth had been wrapped in fabric that trapped moisture.\n\nMereana took notes.\n\n\"Will it recover?\" I asked.\n\n\"Possibly. We need to examine it properly.\"\n\nThe workshop steward offered to pay for a repair.\n\nMereana nodded.\n\n\"That's part of it. The other part is changing the handling process.\"\n\nOne of the visiting artists unfolded a travel schedule. Their next paid engagement was already booked. Because the panels had been stored incorrectly, they would miss a boat connection and have to arrange another.\n\nPip stared at the numbers. \"I didn't realize you had another booking.\"\n\n\"We didn't come here to be available indefinitely,\" Mereana said. \"The workshop needs to discuss the extra costs with its owners. Not ask our performers to absorb them.\"\n\nMaui planted a hand on the doorframe. \"I could carry the whole collection to the next port! Faster than any boat! Unbelievably handsome courier, too.\"\n\nThe artist looked at the warped panel. \"Can you guarantee it won't take any more strain?\"\n\n\"These hands have hauled islands out of the ocean.\"\n\n\"That wasn't the question,\" I said.\n\nHe gave me a wounded look. Then, to Mereana: \"Fine. Who's moving it? And how?\"\n\n\"Nobody moves anything until we agree on the supports.\"\n\nMaui stepped aside so the artist carrying the measuring tools could pass. Pip crossed the imaginary performance off the public notice and started a separate expense sheet, rather than putting out a jar marked *entertainment*.\n\n\"My great heroic destiny today,\" Maui muttered to me, \"is to keep a doorway clear.\"\n\n\"Want a medal?\"\n\n\"Naturally. But lunch will do.\"\n\nMereana heard him and snorted. Her group still didn't owe the village a performance. The expenses, the panel and the next decision remained theirs.\n\nShe asked for a revised lending form that named who could move the work, under what conditions, and how the owners would be contacted. Neri agreed to help draft it. Tamsin volunteered to make a better storage rack, but only after Mereana approved the design.\n\nMaui carried the old rack outside.\n\nHe didn't make a speech.\n\nI found him by the door, watching the group examine the panels.\n\n\"Still wish it were a festival?\" I asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"I like hearing people share beautiful things. I just keep forgetting that choosing not to share is part of the beauty too.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nMereana accepted the tea, then asked whether the chairs could be moved to somewhere the wind wouldn't blow dust into the cups. We carried them into the workshop's small courtyard, where a canvas awning had been pulled taut between two posts.\n\n\"I was worried you'd leave angry,\" Maui admitted.\n\n\"I was angry,\" she answered. \"I also wanted the panels checked. Both things can be true.\"\n\nHe looked at her over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"The work means something to the people who made it,\" she continued. \"Some of them are here, some couldn't travel. I was entrusted to speak about the loan. That doesn't make me the owner of everyone's story.\"\n\nNeri returned with a draft form. Mereana read the first page and pointed out that it included permission to photograph the panels for publicity. Her group had agreed to conservation photographs, not promotional images.\n\nNeri crossed out the sentence and wrote two separate options.\n\nMaui watched. \"I thought the village would be honored to display them.\"\n\n\"It was,\" Mereana said. \"But honor isn't the same as permission.\"\n\nWhen she excused herself to speak to the other artists, he slumped in his chair.\n\n\"I wanted them to feel celebrated.\"\n\n\"I know. Next time ask what celebration they want.\"\n\nHe laughed without much humor. \"Your next sentence is going to be that I don't get to choose the music.\"\n\n\"You can choose what music you play yourself.\"\n\nHe considered that, then drew a small circle on the table with his fingertip.\n\n\"Will you still dance with me after I embarrass myself?\"\n\n\"If you ask me and I want to.\"\n\nHe looked up, relief plain on his face. \"I will ask.\"\n\nWe helped carry the repaired rack back inside after Mereana approved its position. She checked the supports herself. The next morning's signed form would matter more than anything Maui could have announced from a stage, but the repaired storage space was a kind of welcome too.\n\nA little later Mereana joined us outside. She seemed tired, but not angry enough to leave immediately.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. And a chair.\"\n\nI brought both.\n\nShe sat beneath the awning and watched the harbor.\n\n\"I don't mind people being curious,\" she said. \"I mind when curiosity arrives with a costume already picked out for me.\"\n\nMaui looked at his hands.\n\n\"I've done that to people.\"\n\n\"We all can,\" she said. \"The question is whether we notice, and what we do afterward.\"\n\nI thought of my own missing memories, the way strangers sometimes decided who I must have been from a few fragments. I understood the shape of her frustration without pretending the histories were the same.\n\nMereana turned toward me.\n\n\"How is the new garden?\"\n\n\"Small. Salty. Stubborn.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a place worth seeing.\"\n\nWe walked there together after the storage inspection. Fen explained the beds and how visitors could contribute without importing plants that damaged the dunes. Mereana was delighted by the little flowers growing untouched beside the signal post.\n\n\"Those were here before the beds?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Good. I like a place that leaves room for what was already living.\"\n\nBy sunset, the visiting group had decided to stay one more day to work on the storage agreement. They did not perform. Nobody pressured them.\n\nMaui cooked supper. Mereana told us about a boat repair she had supervised back home, mostly to complain about how much easier it was to give advice than to keep a project on budget. Maui confessed he was learning the same thing.\n\nShe laughed.\n\nWhen the meal ended, she stood and stretched.\n\n\"Thank you for asking what we wanted,\" she said.\n\nI looked at the untouched instruments beside her bag.\n\n\"Thank you for trusting us enough to tell us no.\"\n\nAfter she left, Maui and I washed the cups.\n\n\"Was that a successful visit?\" he asked.\n\n\"She got her work inspected, the process changed, and she chose to stay. I'd say yes.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Can I sing?\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him before he could begin anyway.\n\nThe following morning, the lending form had new boxes for consent to move, display, repair, and return the panels. None were checked by default.\n\nMereana read it, made three corrections, and signed only the parts she'd agreed to.\n\nThat was the kind of welcome worth keeping.","word_count":1907},"maui-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Keeper's Refusal","content":"Neri told us the truth without looking at either of us.\n\nThey sat in the archive's west room, where the burned ledger rested under protective cloth. The door was closed, but not locked. Severus had offered to stay and had accepted Neri's request that he wait outside.\n\nIt was the first time Neri had asked me to hear something without calling it a meeting.\n\nMaui sat beside me. He'd come because I wanted company, not because the old keepers belonged to his legend.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"I watched someone turn a traveler away during the Closed Bells,\" they said.\n\nThe room seemed to tighten.\n\n\"How old were you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Old enough to understand the words. Young enough to believe an official would know better than I did.\"\n\nThey looked at the ledger.\n\n\"The keeper said the channels were unstable. He said opening the door might endanger everyone. A woman wanted to leave for her homeworld. She had family waiting. She begged him to let her pass.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI heard the sea outside, impossibly distant from Moonroot Archive.\n\n\"What happened to her?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nNeri's voice broke on the last word.\n\nThey took a breath.\n\n\"The keeper ordered the arch sealed. He used the language of safety and protection. He spoke as if refusing her was an act of compassion. I stood there and did nothing.\"\n\nMaui's hand tightened on his knee.\n\nI wanted to say that Neri had been a child, that they hadn't been responsible, that remembering was enough. But the woman had still been turned away. Her family had still waited on the other side.\n\n\"I don't know what you could have done then,\" I said. \"I do know what you can do now.\"\n\nNeri looked up.\n\n\"Tell the truth in the record,\" I said. \"Help us change the rules that allowed it. And don't ask the people affected to make you feel forgiven.\"\n\nThey looked as though I had struck them.\n\n\"I wasn't going to—\"\n\n\"I know,\" I said softly. \"But I think you've been carrying this alone because you wanted a version where your silence made sense.\"\n\nThey stared at the table.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe word was almost inaudible.\n\nWe stayed a while. I asked what Neri remembered about the keeper's instructions, the weather, and the public notice. I did not ask for a name Neri had already said they couldn't reliably provide. Their memory could be a witness to an action without being proof of the scratched signature in the ledger.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nAt one point Neri said, \"I thought he was trying to keep us safe.\"\n\nMaui shifted in his chair.\n\n\"I've told people I was protecting them,\" he said. \"Sometimes I was. Sometimes I was just frightened of being blamed if something went wrong.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"What did you do?\"\n\n\"Things I was wrong about. Spectacularly wrong. I still tried to give them a good ending.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the answer into a story about his greatness.\n\nHe closed one enormous hand around his hook.\n\n\"I hate this kind of room,\" he said. \"Usually the villain is standing right there when I make my entrance. Excellent entrance, by the way. Very memorable.\"\n\nNeri looked at him. \"What would you do if you found the keeper?\"\n\n\"Ask why he locked somebody in.\"\n\n\"And if he said he was protecting everyone?\"\n\nMaui shifted in the chair. He had faced seas taller than the square's rooftops without blinking. This question bothered him more.\n\n\"Want proof. Probably want to shout.\"\n\n\"That isn't a plan.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"It's the first five seconds of a pretty bad one.\"\n\nThey nearly smiled.\n\nOutside, the tide still had to be measured. The sealed lower passage could not be opened by the great Maui declaring himself useful. Neri had not asked for a rescue, only for a witness to listen.\n\n\"I'm excellent at making impossible things sound easy,\" Maui said. \"You don't owe anybody a more exciting version of what happened. And if they insist, send them to me.\"\n\n\"To frighten them?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"To bore them with a six-hour story about myself. Much crueler.\"\n\nNeri let out a breath that was almost a laugh. The ledger remained on the table, unchanged.\n\nLater, Severus returned and asked whether Neri wanted their account documented as a signed statement, an anonymous summary, or kept private while they considered it. They chose a signed statement with restricted access until the wording could be reviewed.\n\nOrin prepared the form.\n\nI stood beside Neri while they read it.\n\nThe final paragraph did not identify the keeper by name. It recorded what Neri had witnessed: a traveler requesting departure, an official refusing it, and a claimed safety reason that had not been documented with an alternative plan.\n\nNeri signed.\n\nThen they put down the pen and wept.\n\nI did not know whether to embrace them. I asked.\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI held them for a moment, feeling how thin their shoulders were beneath the old coat. My own throat hurt. I wanted the world to be the kind of place where admitting wrong made the consequence disappear.\n\nIt wasn't.\n\nTwo days passed before Neri asked Orin for a second copy of the testimony.\n\nWe were in Moonroot's sorting room, where scraps of damaged paper waited in shallow trays. Orin had labeled each tray by the document it came from, not by the conclusion somebody hoped it would support.\n\n\"I've been reading what I signed,\" Neri said. \"I didn't remember whether the traveler was alone.\"\n\nOrin stopped writing. \"Then we can amend the statement.\"\n\n\"Would that make the whole thing unreliable?\"\n\n\"It would make the amendment visible,\" Orin said. \"That's different.\"\n\nNeri sat down. I could see their hands shaking around the edge of the chair. Maui brought a cup of water and set it nearby without making them take it.\n\n\"I kept seeing the child's red sleeve,\" Neri said. \"The traveler might have been holding their hand. Or the child might have stood beside the official. I can't make the memory settle.\"\n\nNobody offered to tell them which version sounded more likely.\n\nOrin recorded the uncertainty in an addendum and preserved the original wording alongside it. Neri could still choose whether the statement was read publicly. The restricted copy would keep both versions, with the date of the correction and the reason for it.\n\n\"I thought remembering would make me feel less frightened,\" Neri admitted.\n\n\"Sometimes remembering gives you more than you know how to use,\" I said.\n\nMaui shifted on the other side of the table. \"I hate this part,\" he said. \"No monster to punch. No glorious entrance. Just the thing you did, staring at you until you stop trying to make it sound better.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb along the curve of his hook before speaking again.\n\n\"I could talk an island into calling a mistake a victory,\" he said, a crooked grin appearing. \"Doesn't change what happened. Moana was *very* good at reminding me of that. Usually while I was in the middle of my best speech.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"You can tell those parts?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" His grin was quick and painful. \"Depends who's listening. The tattoos are brutal critics.\"\n\nMini Maui popped up and pointed at a dark little image farther across his chest, one Maui rarely called attention to. He covered it with his hand. I wasn't going to ask him to turn his history into entertainment for this meeting.\n\n\"The missing line matters,\" he told Neri. \"I'm good at making stories sound wonderful. Doesn't make every part true.\"\n\nIt was more honest than one of his polished lessons. His pride hadn't disappeared. Neither had his guilt. He could still be a storyteller without making the archive's evidence follow his preferred ending.\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"Did you?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. And sometimes I only learned I should have listened earlier.\"\n\nThe answer made Neri breathe a little more easily.\n\nBefore we left, Orin showed us where the public copy would omit the child's details. The testimony was about a forced refusal of passage. Nobody needed to turn a half-remembered child into a clue for strangers to hunt.\n\nOn the walk to the square, Maui reached for my hand. I accepted, even though I was still thinking about the scarred page and Neri's face when they said they weren't sure.\n\n\"You're allowed not to know everything,\" he said.\n\n\"So are you.\"\n\n\"Terrible rule. It spoils all my dramatic speeches.\"\n\n\"Maybe one or two of them will survive.\"\n\nHe grinned. It felt good to laugh without making the day feel smaller.\n\nAt the next council session, Neri gave permission to read the verified portion of their testimony. The room listened. A woman in the audience asked whether other witnesses could be contacted without being pressured to relive what had happened.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"And they may refuse.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether the old keeper had meant well.\n\nI could feel the room searching for an easier ending.\n\nI stood.\n\n\"Intention matters to understanding how decisions were made,\" I said. \"It doesn't make a locked exit harmless. A person who wasn't allowed to leave was still denied a choice.\"\n\nThe council approved a review of every emergency crossing procedure. Volunteers would be trained to explain safe options and record only the information necessary to help. The lower service passage would stay closed until the structural team could inspect it; closure of an unsafe maintenance tunnel would never be used to justify blocking the independent traveler Threadways.\n\nThat distinction took an entire afternoon to write correctly.\n\nAfterward, Maui and I walked to the cove.\n\n\"I wanted to shout,\" he admitted.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't.\"\n\n\"I wanted to.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\"\n\n\"Because Neri wasn't asking me to make them feel good. They were trying to say something difficult. I wanted to hear it without turning their shame into my performance.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe light had grown gold over the sea-glass orchard. I could smell wet earth and the faint spice of rosemary. The little bench stood empty.\n\nWe sat.\n\n\"I was wrong about many things,\" Maui said. \"Still am, probably.\"\n\n\"Very probably.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"Do you think Neri's testimony will help?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Not because it ends the question. Because people can now ask better questions.\"\n\nHe put his hand over mine.\n\nWe watched a boat make the safe turn toward the upper landing, its lantern moving steadily through the evening light.\n\nThe harbor did not become innocent because somebody had spoken.\n\nBut the words were out in the open.\n\nThat mattered.","word_count":1773},"maui-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Last Big Performance","content":"The invitation was written in letters three inches high.\n\nMAUI, HERO OF THE TIDE, WILL OPEN THE REPAIR CELEBRATION.\n\nHe stared at the poster with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Well,\" I said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\nI waited for him to lift his chin and begin planning a speech about himself.\n\nInstead he asked, \"Who decided that?\"\n\nPip appeared from behind the lantern table.\n\n\"The entertainment committee. They thought people would like something exciting after all the repairs.\"\n\nMaui looked around the square.\n\nHis grin flickered over the poster. For a breath I saw the old hunger in his face: the gigantic letters, the crowd calling his name, everybody waiting to see what he would do.\n\nMini Maui struck a heroic pose on his chest and began counting imaginary cheers.\n\nMaui covered him with one hand.\n\n\"Okay,\" he muttered. \"I am not going to pretend this isn't flattering.\"\n\nPip raised an eyebrow. \"But?\"\n\n\"But somebody's got to carry the benches, the kids have their show, and I am fairly certain Fen will throw me into the sea if I set off fireworks near the rigging.\"\n\n\"You could still introduce the event,\" I said.\n\nMaui considered it. \"I could. With a dramatic speech, a magnificent transformation, perhaps an interpretive dance—\"\n\n\"No fireworks,\" Pip interrupted.\n\n\"Your people are relentless.\"\n\nHe laughed, and I loved that he could admit wanting the spotlight without making it the condition for helping. He was still a demigod who liked to hear his own name. The difference was that he could let other names be said beside it.\n\n\"Tell me where the benches go,\" he said. \"Then we'll talk about the interpretive dance.\"\n\nThe temporary surface channel had passed its second inspection. Riverstone's bridge was almost ready for its final load test. The western Starwake boards had reopened, and the Brasswatch generator had stopped behaving like an offended dragon. The village had survived a string of storms and repairs, but the lower service passage remained closed while engineers studied its age and the old decisions surrounding it.\n\nThere was reason to celebrate.\n\nThere was also a lot of work that didn't belong to one famous man.\n\n\"I want that poster framed,\" Maui said. \"Possibly in a larger frame. Have you seen the lettering?\"\n\nPip blinked. \"So you'll open?\"\n\nHe stared at the children's program, then at the empty space for the repair crew's names.\n\n\"I want to. That's the problem. I can hear myself stealing the morning before they've even begun.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI didn't answer for him.\n\n\"Ask the children if they want an introduction,\" he said. \"If they do, I'll make it their big entrance. If they don't, I'll heroically survive sitting down.\"\n\nThe disappointment was real. I could see it cross Pip's face. They had hoped for a draw, something that would make the square full and the donation box heavier.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"I can help with something else,\" he offered. \"If you need it.\"\n\nPip looked toward the pile of lantern hooks.\n\n\"Actually...\"\n\nWe spent the next two days preparing the celebration.\n\nOn the first afternoon, I found Maui behind the stack of stage screens arguing with a draft program. He'd drawn himself three times larger than the performers.\n\n\"It's marketing,\" he said. \"You lead with your strongest feature.\"\n\n\"The strongest feature is a festival everybody can attend.\"\n\n\"Second strongest.\"\n\nEdda came past with the approved route plan. The space beside the ramp needed to stay open even if a crowd moved suddenly toward the lantern display. Maui offered to stand there and direct everybody.\n\n\"You're the thing they'd rush toward,\" Edda said.\n\nHe looked delighted, which proved her point.\n\n\"Could you carry these spare screens into the west storage room?\" she asked. \"Without announcing it from the stage?\"\n\n\"The cruelty here is astounding.\"\n\nHe carried them, although he made a show of tiptoeing under their weight. At the doorway he tried to salute, bumped his shoulder against the lintel and glared at the building as though it had challenged him personally.\n\nEdda checked the cleared turning area. \"Better.\"\n\n\"Remember this day,\" Maui told me. \"I saved a festival with a doorway.\"\n\n\"I'll have it engraved on something tiny.\"\n\n\"Nothing tiny could contain the legend.\"\n\nHe kept the ridiculous draft program folded in his shirt. I didn't ask him to throw it away. Pip's real program could still belong to everybody else.\n\nGreg would play a short original set, because he had chosen to and could leave when it was over. The children from Tidewake wanted to perform their little story about Fen falling into the harbor. Fen protested until they discovered they had been given the role of the wise crab.\n\nIroh offered tea. Mara brought bread. Tamsin made sure the decorations were mounted on independent supports rather than the ancient arches. Edda checked the viewing areas so people could sit or stand without blocking the routes.\n\nMaui helped Pip sort hooks.\n\nIt was possibly the least glamorous work he'd ever done.\n\nHe was very bad at sorting.\n\n\"Those are the long ones,\" Pip told him.\n\n\"They look short.\"\n\n\"Compared with your hand, everything looks short.\"\n\nI laughed from the next table.\n\nMaui held up a hook.\n\n\"This one?\"\n\n\"Medium.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of hook?\"\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of fish?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Excellent point.\"\n\nOn the evening before the festival, the volunteers met to confirm what the celebration actually required. Pip had written a cheerful program, but the schedule had no break between the children's kite display and the lantern procession. Greg was listed for three songs despite having agreed to two.\n\n\"He'll still do three,\" somebody said. \"He likes playing.\"\n\n\"Ask him,\" I said.\n\nWe found Greg sorting cables by the stage. He was happy to play two. The third song wasn't ready, and he'd promised to be back in Beach City the next morning. Nobody was entitled to turn a friendly offer into an obligation.\n\nPip revised the program without grumbling. The children chose a shorter kite demonstration because Fen had posted an afternoon wind warning. Tamsin checked that the lantern stands could be moved without blocking Edda's turning space. Mara counted the bread orders against the bakery's actual flour supply.\n\nMaui carried chairs while we worked. When one volunteer asked whether he could still give a short heroic introduction, he stopped and looked at the children waiting to test their lantern hooks.\n\n\"I'd rather introduce the people who did the repairs,\" he said. \"If they want to speak.\"\n\nSeveral of them didn't. The council decided a name could go on the public thanks list only with permission; private volunteers could be thanked collectively. One worker asked not to be identified at all. Pip crossed the name out without asking why.\n\nLater Maui admitted that he'd imagined the crowd chanting for him.\n\n\"You can want applause,\" I said. \"You just can't collect it on someone else's behalf.\"\n\n\"I know. I just really wanted them to chant my name. In rhythm. Then I felt like an idiot for wanting it.\"\n\n\"It doesn't. You look ridiculous when you try to hide behind a basket of lantern hooks.\"\n\nHe peered over the basket. \"Then my disguise has failed.\"\n\nI laughed and kissed him on the cheek. There was no grand lesson written on the program, only accurate times, safe paths, and people who'd agreed to be there.\n\nPip brought the revised poster round after sunset, its corners curling where the ink was still wet.\n\n\"Could you look before I hang it?\" they asked Maui.\n\nHe held it at arm's length. The words were smaller than before. The children's performance sat at the top, followed by the inspection-fund supper and the lantern walk. No one's name occupied half the paper.\n\nMaui swallowed.\n\n\"Are you disappointed?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe took much too long to answer. \"A little.\"\n\nPip's mouth tightened. I thought they might apologize, though they hadn't done anything wrong.\n\n\"I used to like seeing my name,\" Maui said. \"That isn't your responsibility.\"\n\n\"It's okay to have a name on the poster,\" Pip replied. \"You were helping with the benches.\"\n\nHe looked down at the volunteer list, where his name stood between Lio and Edda. \"It seems very small.\"\n\n\"So is mine,\" Pip said. \"I painted six of them.\"\n\nThat made him laugh. Pip did not join in until he stopped. Then they asked whether he would carry the sign to the square, because the paint was wet and he had steady hands.\n\nI watched him take it carefully by the wooden frame instead of holding it over his head like a victory banner.\n\nLater, while we walked through the lantern-lit market, Maui caught my sleeve.\n\n\"I know you love other people,\" he said. \"I know I'm not supposed to turn this into a competition. But I miss being somebody everyone looks for.\"\n\n\"Do you want me to pretend I don't see you?\"\n\n\"No.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I want you to notice the parts nobody else looks for.\"\n\nI thought of the way he'd carried the poster so Pip's paint wouldn't smudge. Of his quiet face when a child asked a question he couldn't answer. I told him both.\n\nHis smile arrived slowly, uncertain at the edges.\n\n\"That feels different,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe leaned close, then hesitated because people were walking past with armfuls of chairs. I pulled him aside by the lantern shed and kissed him, deliberately and a little awkwardly, with the smell of wet paint still on his hands. We laughed when a hook rattled above us. He didn't ask whether anyone had seen.\n\nBy festival morning, the poster had been replaced. It listed the schedule without turning any person into the center of the world. Maui's name appeared under *volunteer setup*, alongside Pip, Lio, Edda, and a dozen others.\n\nHe looked at it for a long moment.\n\n\"Do you regret it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"I like being seen.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I think I always will.\"\n\n\"That's not a crime.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"I just don't want to need it to know who I am.\"\n\nWe stood together beneath the lanterns.\n\nThen Lio shouted for help with the stage ramp, and Maui went to ask what they needed.\n\nThe celebration began late because the first tray of bread had to be rescued from an overenthusiastic oven. Nobody minded. Aang helped the children launch a few kites from the safe ridge, keeping sacred Air Nomad traditions separate from a local craft festival. Toph called the whole event \"terribly sentimental\" and then refused to leave before the last performance.\n\nGreg played a song about a ferry bell.\n\nIt was quieter than the crowd expected.\n\nPeople listened.\n\nI saw Maui standing at the edge of the square, holding a spare lantern pole in one hand. He wasn't watching the stage. He was watching a child who couldn't quite reach the hook they'd been assigned.\n\nHe knelt, held the pole steady, and asked whether they wanted help.\n\nThe child nodded.\n\nTogether they hung the lantern.\n\nNo applause.\n\nNo one even turned to look.\n\nSomething in my chest ached.\n\nLater, when the crowd began moving toward the food stalls, Maui came to find me.\n\n\"Did I miss anything?\"\n\n\"The wise crab was excellent.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Greg played beautifully.\"\n\n\"I heard.\"\n\n\"And you helped Pip.\"\n\nHe shrugged.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I saw you with the lantern.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\n\"You did?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"Was it all right?\"\n\n\"It was lovely.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\nI took his hand and pulled him behind the sheltered garden wall, out of the flow of people. The lanterns made soft gold patterns on the stone.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHis smile was sudden and warm.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nI kissed him. His hands settled carefully at my waist, and for a few moments the festival noise became a distant, pleasant blur. We laughed against each other when a paper fish caught in his hair.\n\n\"Your audience has arrived,\" I whispered.\n\n\"A terrible critic.\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nWe rejoined the crowd after the lantern keeper checked the final hooks. The donation box held enough to fund part of the next inspection, and the public ledger recorded the amount. No one promised the service tunnel would reopen because the celebration had gone well.\n\nAt the end, Pip thanked the volunteers.\n\nMaui was named with everyone else.\n\nHe clapped for the children.\n\nWhen we walked home, he carried no trophy.\n\nOnly a small bent hook he'd rescued from the sorting pile, because he thought we could use it in the garden.\n\nWe couldn't.\n\nI let him keep it anyway.","word_count":2125}},"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-28":{"number":28,"title":"The Game We Change","content":"I beat Iroh at Pai Sho because he underestimated how stubborn I could be.\n\nHe would later claim that he had been studying an advanced strategy. I would later point out that his advanced strategy involved staring at the wrong tile for an entire minute.\n\nThe board sat between us in the Jasmine Dragon's back room. Rain had cleared the street, leaving the paving wet and dark. Mina had gone home on time. The shop's accounts were finished. For once, nobody was waiting for Iroh to be a general, a mentor or a supplier of tea.\n\n\"Your move,\" I said.\n\n\"I am considering.\"\n\n\"You've been considering long enough for the moon to change its mind.\"\n\nHe smiled and moved the white lotus tile. I took it with a move he'd shown me the week before.\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\n\"That,\" he said, \"was not polite.\"\n\n\"You taught me the rule.\"\n\n\"I did not expect you to remember it so enthusiastically.\"\n\nWe played on. He told me stories between turns, and I knew enough about him now to recognize when he was using one to distract me from the board. A story about a merchant who tried to sell fake jade lasted exactly as long as it took him to set up a trap on the far side.\n\nI saw the trap.\n\n\"You know,\" I said, \"telling me a story while setting a trap doesn't make the trap invisible.\"\n\n\"An observation of great insight.\"\n\n\"Stop flattering me. It gives you away.\"\n\nHe laughed and, for the first time all evening, actually looked surprised by my next move.\n\nWe had planned a relaxed game. Then a knock sounded at the rear door. Ren, the White Lotus scholar, stood in the hallway with a folded note.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" she said. \"The message arrived late.\"\n\nIroh took the note but did not open it immediately. \"Is anyone in danger?\"\n\n\"Not at present. The next meeting needs a new room.\"\n\n\"Then I will answer in the morning.\"\n\nRen nodded. She had learned to ask; he had learned that an invitation wasn't a command. Once she left, he put the note face down beside the kettle.\n\nWhen Ren had gone, Iroh unfolded the note. The White Lotus seal sat crooked on the paper.\n\n\"The proposed room has only one exit,\" he said.\n\n\"You know that from an address?\"\n\n\"I inspected the building years ago.\" He pulled a spare sheet over and drew stairs, a rear alley and an alternative hall. The map appeared with the certainty of a man who'd commanded more than tea kettles. \"The council should know better.\"\n\n\"The Jasmine Dragon closes at six tomorrow,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"I am aware of my own business hours.\"\n\n\"Mina isn't staying late to serve your secret meeting.\"\n\nHis pencil stopped. \"I hadn't asked her.\"\n\n\"You were about to.\"\n\n\"I was considering being persuasive.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\n\"No.\" He put the pencil down. \"It is an unattractive habit.\"\n\nHe folded the map. \"The delegates may bring their own refreshments. Mina will finish her paid shift. In the morning I shall ask about the other hall.\"\n\n\"You could have told Ren that.\"\n\n\"Where would be the pleasure in drawing an unnecessary map?\"\n\nHe slid it beneath the Pai Sho board. \"Now, I believe you've left your flank exposed.\"\n\n\"I didn't know Pai Sho had flanks.\"\n\n\"Everything has flanks, if one has been foolish enough to spend a career looking for them.\"\n\nHe smiled. There was affection in it, but an old general's irritation too. I moved before he could enjoy his advantage.\n\n\"Are you angry with her?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. She had a reasonable request.\"\n\n\"And you said no.\"\n\n\"Not no. Tomorrow.\"\n\nI glanced at the board. \"A very advanced move.\"\n\n\"One I have been practicing.\"\n\nWe went back to the game. He took my best piece and apologized so extravagantly that I suspected another trick. I leaned across the table to see the far corner and noticed him watching me rather than the tiles.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You look happy.\"\n\n\"That's because I'm winning.\"\n\n\"Ah. So that is what happiness looks like.\"\n\n\"It's a rare experience for you.\"\n\nHe placed one hand over his heart. \"Cruel.\"\n\nIroh moved a tile toward the edge, then stopped with his fingertip still resting on it.\n\n\"May I change my mind?\" he asked.\n\n\"About the move?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Before you've released it, of course.\"\n\nHe drew it back. \"An excellent rule for a board game. Less useful in warfare.\"\n\n\"Fortunately we're playing Pai Sho and not conquering a city.\"\n\nFor a moment the joke went wrong. I saw the old general behind his smile and wished I'd been gentler. He looked at the pieces, then up at me.\n\n\"That is exactly why I enjoy playing with you,\" he said. \"We may correct a move without someone getting hurt.\"\n\nI let out the breath I'd been holding.\n\nHe told me about a game he'd once played against an officer who was afraid to win. Every move had been designed to flatter, and the board had become a tedious performance of loyalty.\n\n\"Did you tell him?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I was angry that he was boring and did not ask why he was afraid.\"\n\n\"Would you act differently now?\"\n\n\"I hope so. But I can't make the past game fair simply by regretting it.\"\n\nWe played in silence for a while. I was startled by how much I wanted him to win honestly, not because he needed proof of his skill, but because I wanted to see him light up at a clever move.\n\nHe caught me watching.\n\n\"Do you intend to distract me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\n\"An underhanded tactic.\"\n\n\"Is it working?\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe smiled, then caught one of my pieces. I groaned so loudly that Mina called from the stairwell to ask whether a teapot had fallen. We assured her the shop was intact.\n\nWhen we finished, Iroh offered me his hand across the board.\n\n\"Would you like to play again someday?\"\n\n\"Yes. On a day neither of us feels obliged to win.\"\n\n\"Then we may finally learn something.\"\n\nI leaned closer, my answer to the invitation plain in the way my fingers curled around his. The kiss waited until the game was done. That made it feel less like a prize and more like something we had both wanted all along.\n\nI wanted to kiss the smile off his face, which was inconvenient because he was doing his best to win. The moment made me think of the first time I'd sat in this room and mistaken his warm patience for the absence of loneliness. He could be wise and silly and competitive. I liked the silly part more than I'd expected.\n\nThe board grew crowded. Iroh could have won in three moves if I'd followed his usual pattern. Instead I traded a piece he considered valuable for a route he'd left open.\n\nHe stared.\n\n\"Did you mean to do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You gave up the center.\"\n\n\"I wanted the outside.\"\n\nHe leaned back, then laughed properly. \"You are very difficult to teach.\"\n\n\"Try being loved by me.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nI felt heat in my cheeks. \"That's not fair. You can't say something like that when I'm trying to concentrate.\"\n\n\"Perhaps I have developed a strategy after all.\"\n\nWe finished the game. I won by a narrow margin, and he made me recount the move that had changed it. He listened as though it mattered. I didn't need him to pretend to lose, and I didn't need to win to be sure he enjoyed my company.\n\nAs we packed the tiles, he asked about the people I would visit next week.\n\n\"I'll be going through the Threadway on Thursday,\" I said. \"I've already told you the days. Do you want a note when I arrive?\"\n\n\"Only if you wish to send one. I would like to know you're safe. I do not need an account of every person you see.\"\n\nI nodded. Our agreement about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory wasn't a single confession that solved everything. It was a pattern of choices, like returning to a game after an argument and discovering you could still surprise each other.\n\nIroh held out the last tile. \"One more round?\"\n\n\"Are you prepared to lose again?\"\n\n\"Not in the slightest.\"\n\nI set the box aside. \"Then I'm going to claim my prize first.\"\n\n\"And what is that?\"\n\n\"A kiss, if you want one.\"\n\nHis smile changed. \"Very much.\"\n\nHe met me halfway across the table. We bumped the kettle with our elbows and had to stop laughing before we tried again. The kiss that followed was quiet enough to make the laughter seem far away.\n\nLater we went upstairs and closed the door. The board remained on the table, unfinished for the next game. In the morning, I found the white lotus tile at the center and a note in Iroh's careful hand: *A rematch is requested. No tricks promised.*\n\nI put it in my pocket and went downstairs to help open the shop.","word_count":1511}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Work of Staying","content":"The application had been sitting under the car wash receipts for two days.\n\nGreg wasn't snooping. He was looking for the electricity bill, which was somehow more embarrassing. He lifted the envelope, saw my name, and put it back on the table without opening it.\n\n\"That looks important,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Want to tell me?\"\n\nI looked at the plastic chair across from him. I wanted to say it wasn't decided, which was true, and leave the rest until I knew what I wanted. But he would notice me being careful with every word.\n\n\"I got offered a job,\" I said. \"Not here.\"\n\nHe smiled immediately. \"That's fantastic.\"\n\n\"It's across the water. I'd have to spend longer stretches away from Beach City.\"\n\n\"Still fantastic.\"\n\nThe fluorescent light buzzed above us. I watched him straighten a stack of receipts that didn't need straightening.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You can be happy for me and still have feelings about the distance.\"\n\nHe stopped moving. \"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why are you pretending this is as easy as finding a new parking spot?\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Because I was afraid if I said I'd miss you, it'd sound like I was asking you to give it up.\"\n\nThere it was. The thing beneath the joke. I pulled out the other chair.\n\n\"Sit?\"\n\nHe sat. The electricity bill fell from his hand and landed upside down between us.\n\n\"I'm not asking permission,\" I said. \"I am asking whether you want to talk about what this would mean for us.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe started with the stupidly practical parts because they were the parts we could actually do something about. The job paid enough to cover travel if I didn't spend it all getting back and forth for one-night visits. Greg had bookings on certain weekends, including a school fundraiser he'd already promised to play. I had other partners and commitments beyond Beach City; none of those disappeared because a job offer existed.\n\n\"I know you've been clear about being ethically non-monogamous,\" he said. \"And that you prefer polyamory. I guess I keep thinking a schedule's gonna make me sound like I'm booking a slot in your life.\"\n\n\"You are in my life. A schedule just means I don't promise to be in two places on the same Thursday.\"\n\nHe laughed softly. \"That'd make a good song.\"\n\n\"Only if you leave the receipts out of it.\"\n\nWe wrote down what we could promise. We would tell each other about changes early enough to matter. Neither of us would treat silence as a punishment. We could say we wanted more time without using guilt to get it. Visits would be invitations, not surprise tests. If either of us couldn't make something work, we would talk about it instead of announcing a failure.\n\nWhen we finished the first page of our plan, I went to the board by the sink and wrote down the dates I'd have to answer the job offer. I needed an actual deadline, not one I could move whenever the decision made me nervous.\n\nGreg looked at it. \"So there's an interview?\"\n\n\"Yes. A second one. I didn't tell you because I didn't know whether I wanted to go.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"I want to find out what the work is like when nobody's trying to impress me.\"\n\nHe thought about that, then nodded.\n\nWe made a list of questions I could ask without surrendering the chance to accept. Would the job pay for travel when I needed to cross the water? Who approved a schedule change? Would I be expected to take on duties that belonged to people already working there? If I chose to leave after the trial period, would I still be able to come back without being treated as a traitor?\n\nGreg laughed at the last one. \"That sounds like a lot of your life.\"\n\n\"Also sounds like I should've checked the bus timetable before offering to drive you every weekend,\" Greg said.\n\nHe pulled a car-wash receipt across the desk and did sums on the back. Fuel, the van's increasingly unreliable starter, his own shifts and the days Steven might need him. No grand promises. Just ugly arithmetic.\n\n\"Two trips, maybe three,\" he said. \"More if the van figures out how to become a boat.\"\n\n\"I didn't ask you to be my ferry.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I know.\" He tapped four fingers on the paper, paused, and tried a different rhythm. \"I do this thing where I volunteer before I check whether I can show up.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I had a whole speech planned. Super supportive boyfriend, excellent hair, totally cool about distance.\"\n\n\"Your hair wasn't part of the interview.\"\n\n\"Well, it should be.\"\n\nHis grin faded.\n\n\"I don't want you to turn it down,\" he said. \"I also don't want to wave goodbye and spend a month hearing every missed call as a sad song.\"\n\n\"Then don't write the song alone.\"\n\n\"Oh, I started it. It's terrible.\" He pushed the receipt toward me. \"This is better. Boring numbers, real phone calls, me actually showing up.\"\n\nI wrote the interview date beside his fuel estimate, leaving room to change both. Greg returned the electricity bill to the top of his stack; that was what he'd been looking for in the first place.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe stood, opened the little office window, and let in air that smelled of soap and summer asphalt. The car wash machinery had stopped for the day. The silence made the room feel like someplace we could choose to be.\n\n\"Can I tell you what scares me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not that you'll like the job. I hope you do. I think I'm scared I'll start treating your coming home like proof you still love me, and then every delayed bus will make me a jerk.\"\n\nI leaned against the desk. \"Then we have to notice when that starts happening. I can't guarantee I won't miss a visit. I can guarantee I won't make you guess whether I mean to come.\"\n\nHe looked toward the calendar.\n\n\"Could we plan the first week after the interview before either of us promises the whole year?\"\n\n\"That sounds perfect.\"\n\n\"Wow. You said perfect. Write that down.\"\n\nI did, in the margin of the page he was holding. He tried not to smile and failed.\n\nOur non-exclusive relationship wasn't a test of how much distance we could endure. It was two people trying not to let the fear of a future decision take the place of one we could make today.\n\nI asked whether he wanted to walk to the diner before we returned to the application. He did. He held out his hand, and when I took it he looked as pleased as he had the first time we played a song together.\n\nGreg pointed at the page. \"This feels very grown-up.\"\n\n\"Terrifying, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I was hoping love came with fewer columns.\"\n\nI nudged his knee with mine. \"You have a business. You know columns.\"\n\n\"That's different. The columns at the car wash don't have feelings.\"\n\n\"Your accounts would suggest otherwise.\"\n\nHe laughed, then went quiet.\n\n\"I do want you to stay,\" he said. \"I know I don't get to decide for you. But I want you to know that.\"\n\nThe admission made my throat tighten. There was something braver about hearing his preference without having to fight for my freedom first.\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said. \"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And if I take the job, I still want to come home here sometimes. Not because I owe you proof. Because I like this stupid office and your terrible coffee.\"\n\n\"Hey. The coffee is improving.\"\n\n\"It's learning to apologize.\"\n\nHe reached across the table. \"Can I hold your hand?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat with our fingers tangled between the bill and the job application. He didn't ask to see the letter. I didn't hand it over to prove I trusted him.\n\nAfter closing, we walked to the van. The air had turned cool. A light flickered in the car wash sign, and he made a note to call someone about it before the next wet morning.\n\nInside the van, he put on a quiet recording—not one of the old songs that felt too loaded, but a rough instrumental he'd made the previous week. I leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"Stay for dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Tonight.\"\n\n\"Tonight's pretty good.\"\n\nHe kissed me when I turned toward him. We had all the time we wanted for that moment, and no right to assume anything about the next one. Later, we went inside and let the night become ours.\n\nIn the morning, the application was still unanswered. I put it in the drawer beside my toothbrush. We would talk again when I knew more, not pretend a single night could settle a whole future.","word_count":1470}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Things That Remain","content":"We made a list of things Danse knew were his.\n\nNot the Brotherhood’s.\n\nNot the Institute’s.\n\nHis.\n\nThe exercise began because he had been pacing the bunker at two in the morning asking whether liking certain things could have been programmed.\n\n“Probably,” I said.\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“That isn’t helpful.”\n\n“You could say the same about me. Maybe I only like certain foods because of childhood. Maybe I hate certain sounds because of something I forgot. Nobody chooses the starting material.”\n\n“That’s not the same.”\n\n“No. But the obsession with finding a perfectly untouched original self is going to kill you.”\n\nHe stared.\n\nThen sat.\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Fine what?”\n\n“Make your point.”\n\nI found paper.\n\nAt the top I wrote: THINGS DANSE LIKES.\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n“This is childish.”\n\n“Eggs.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\n“Eggs.”\n\nI wrote it down.\n\n“Beer.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Power armor.”\n\n“That’s equipment.”\n\n“You like it.”\n\n“I appreciate its tactical value.”\n\nI wrote POWER ARMOR in larger letters.\n\nDanse muttered something.\n\n“Laser weapons.”\n\n“Properly maintained laser weapons.”\n\n“Terrible coffee.”\n\n“My coffee is fine.”\n\n“Incorrect. Added anyway.”\n\nHe tried to take the pencil.\n\nI moved it out of reach.\n\n“Your turn.”\n\nDanse considered.\n\n“Flying.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“I knew it.”\n\n“Don’t make this unbearable.”\n\n“Continue.”\n\n“Repair work.”\n\n“Teaching.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“Do I?”\n\n“You absolutely do.”\n\nHe thought about the teenager at the settlement.\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Helping people.”\n\n“That’s vague.”\n\n“So is power armor.”\n\n“It is not.”\n\nI wrote it anyway.\n\nThen Danse took the pencil from me.\n\nHe added: ORDER.\n\nAfter a moment: HONESTY.\n\nThen: YOU.\n\nThe pencil stopped.\n\nI stared at the word.\n\nDanse stared at it too.\n\n“I suppose that one could be programming,” he said softly.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nA joke.\n\nA terrible one.\n\nI hit his shoulder with the paper.\n\nHe laughed.\n\n“Asshole.”\n\n“I’m learning.”\n\n“You are.”\n\nI took the pencil back and wrote DANSE beneath the list.\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“What’s that?”\n\n“Something you like eventually.”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“You can’t decide that for me.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI drew a question mark beside it.\n\n“Pending.”\n\nHe looked at the paper for a long time.\n\nThen folded it carefully and put it in his pocket.\n\nThe next morning we went scavenging.\n\nNot because the Brotherhood ordered it.\n\nBecause the settlement needed wiring for a pump and we needed ammunition.\n\nDanse wore combat armor without insignia. He still carried himself like a paladin.\n\nMaybe he always would.\n\nAt an old hardware store, we found three rolls of copper wire, a box of screws, and an intact pre-War coffee maker.\n\nDanse held it up.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You haven’t heard my proposal.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“We could improve morale.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Your coffee is the enemy of morale.”\n\nHe inspected the machine.\n\n“Power requirements are inefficient.”\n\n“That is not a no.”\n\nWe brought it home.\n\nIt took him four hours to rebuild the wiring.\n\nThe first cup it produced was terrible.\n\nI stared at him.\n\nDanse stared at the cup.\n\n“How?” I asked.\n\nHe looked personally betrayed by technology.\n\n“I followed the instructions.”\n\nI laughed until I cried.\n\nHe tried to be offended.\n\nThen started laughing too.\n\nWe ended up sitting on the bunker floor beside a disassembled coffee maker, both of us helpless.\n\nIt was the happiest I had seen him since banishment.\n\nLater, after we repaired it properly, the coffee was almost drinkable.\n\nDanse took a sip.\n\n“Better.”\n\n“Marginally.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter.\n\n“I added something to the list.”\n\n“What?”\n\nHe pulled the folded paper from his pocket.\n\nBeneath DANSE? he had written:\n\nTRYING.\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe looked embarrassed.\n\n“That’s enough for now,” he said.\n\nI crossed the room and kissed him.\n\n“Yes,” I said against his mouth. “It is.”\n\nThe list grew.\n\nNot quickly.\n\nDanse distrusted self-discovery when it did not come with measurable objectives.\n\nBut every few days he added something.\n\nRAIN, eventually, with the qualification *when indoors*.\n\nDOGS, after a settlement mutt slept on his boot for an entire afternoon.\n\nOLD BOOKS, though he crossed out *old* and replaced it with *well-written*.\n\nQUIET.\n\nTEACHING, finally admitted without argument.\n\nOne evening I found him staring at the page with the pencil hovering.\n\n“What?”\n\n“I don’t know how to write this.”\n\n“That has never stopped you from writing a report.”\n\n“This isn’t a report.”\n\n“Exactly. Much higher stakes.”\n\nHe gave me a tired look.\n\nThen wrote: BEING NEEDED.\n\nI read it.\n\n“That makes sense.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis response surprised me.\n\nDanse set the pencil down.\n\n“I don’t think it’s healthy.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I don’t know where usefulness ends and identity begins.”\n\nThat was more insight than he would have tolerated from me a month earlier.\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n“Do you like helping people?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Would you still help them if nobody thanked you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Would you still be worth feeding if you couldn’t repair anything tomorrow?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“That’s different.”\n\n“Is it?”\n\nHe stared at the list.\n\nI let him.\n\nEventually he drew a line beneath BEING NEEDED and wrote something else.\n\nBEING WANTED.\n\nMy throat closed.\n\nDanse looked embarrassed.\n\n“That one is worse.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“It feels selfish.”\n\n“Wanting to be wanted?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI reached across the table and took the pencil.\n\nBelow his entry I wrote: NORMAL.\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“That’s not something I like.”\n\n“No. I’m labeling the feeling.”\n\nHe stared at the word.\n\n“I don’t know what normal is anymore.”\n\n“Good. It was probably overrated.”\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\nWe took the list with us the next day when we went to help repair a pump.\n\nThe pump failed whenever the settlement tried filling the upper storage tank. Danse wanted to remove the entire housing.\n\n\"That'll leave the lower trough dry for half the morning,\" the settlement mechanic said, grease on her wrists. \"Try the intake seal.\"\n\n\"The motor is drawing too much current.\"\n\n\"Because it's pulling air.\"\n\nHe checked the pressure gauge. The needle fell exactly where she said it would.\n\n\"Seal first,\" he said, as though issuing the order made it his idea.\n\nThe mechanic raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Your assessment was correct,\" Danse added.\n\n\"Better sentence.\"\n\nI hid my smile behind the tool bag.\n\nWe isolated the intake, replaced the cracked seal and tested the pump under load. Danse recorded measurements; the mechanic decided when the household taps could reopen. When he reached toward a control without warning, she slapped his wrist away.\n\n\"Not your station.\"\n\nHe stared at her, then stepped back.\n\nAt noon a small leak remained. It was safe enough to monitor, but he wouldn't call the repair done. He marked a return inspection.\n\n\"You can leave that with me,\" she said.\n\n\"I'm aware.\"\n\n\"You don't sound aware.\"\n\n\"I am attempting to express concern without assuming command.\"\n\n\"Try 'thank you.'\"\n\nHis silence lasted a beat too long.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said.\n\nWe both heard the effort in it. He packed the tools anyway.\n\nThe settlement had a dog named Captain who immediately decided Danse belonged to him.\n\nCaptain followed him from the well to the workbench, sat on his feet when he stopped moving, and growled at anyone who approached the tool bag.\n\nDanse regarded the animal with increasing helplessness.\n\n“He’s interfering with operations.”\n\n“He loves you.”\n\n“That is not operationally relevant.”\n\nCaptain rolled onto his back.\n\nDanse looked around to see whether anyone was watching.\n\nEveryone was.\n\nHe scratched the dog’s stomach anyway.\n\nThe settlement children laughed.\n\nDanse turned red.\n\nOne of them asked, “Mister Danse, are you coming back tomorrow?”\n\nHe froze.\n\nNot because of the question.\n\nBecause of the assumption inside it.\n\nTomorrow.\n\nThey expected him to have one.\n\n“Yes,” he said after a moment. “If the replacement valve holds, I’ll check the pressure.”\n\nThe child nodded and ran away.\n\nDanse watched them go.\n\nOn the walk home, he was quiet.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“They expect me back.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That should not matter as much as it does.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\nHe had no answer.\n\nAt Bravo he unfolded the list.\n\nBelow BEING WANTED he added:\n\nCOMING BACK.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That one’s good.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe did not sound uncertain.\n\nThen he pointed at the old question mark beside DANSE.\n\n“Not ready to remove that.”\n\n“You don’t have to.”\n\n“But I’m adding something.”\n\nHe wrote beneath it:\n\nWORTH FINDING OUT.\n\nI turned the paper over and read the entire list again.\n\nEGGS.\n\nBEER.\n\nPOWER ARMOR, underlined because I had been petty.\n\nGOOD TOOLS.\n\nRAIN WHEN INDOORS.\n\nDOGS.\n\nOLD BOOKS, amended to WELL-WRITTEN BOOKS.\n\nTEACHING.\n\nBEING WANTED.\n\nCOMING BACK.\n\nTRYING.\n\nIt was not a personality profile. It was a trail.\n\nDanse tapped the line that said MUSIC.\n\n“Cutler would make fun of this.”\n\n“For liking music?”\n\n“For needing paper to admit it.”\n\n“What would he put on the list?”\n\nDanse smiled despite the ache behind it.\n\n“Annoying me.”\n\nI wrote CUTLER ENJOYED ANNOYING DANSE in the margin.\n\nHe reached for the pencil.\n\n“Give me that.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“That is not about me.”\n\n“It is absolutely about you.”\n\nHe tried to grab the page. I moved it away. For thirty seconds we wrestled over a piece of paper like children until Danse stopped, laughing hard enough that he had to lean against the table.\n\nWhen the laughter faded, he looked at the line again.\n\n“I’m glad I remember him,” he said.\n\nEven if the memories were manufactured, he did not add.\n\nHe did not need to.\n\n“I am too.”\n\nDanse took the pencil back and added another line beneath WORTH FINDING OUT.\n\nMAKING THINGS WORK.\n\n“That one was obvious,” I said.\n\n“I mean more than equipment.”\n\nI went quiet.\n\nHe tapped the words with the eraser.\n\n“Teams. Plans. People, sometimes.”\n\n“You like fixing people?”\n\n“No.” His answer was immediate. “People are not machines.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nMonths ago that distinction would have sounded different coming from him.\n\n“What, then?”\n\n“I can repair a pump and teach someone to check the seal. I can't give an order and make somebody stop being afraid.”\n\n“That is painfully close to wisdom.”\n\n“Don’t make it strange.”\n\n“Too late.”\n\nHe folded the list with unusual care, not because it was official, but because it wasn’t.\n\nNo one had ordered him to become the person described on that page.\n\nFor a while neither of us spoke.\n\nThe coffee maker clicked behind us.\n\nCaptain’s fur clung to Danse’s trousers.\n\nA half-repaired valve waited in his pack for tomorrow.\n\nThe list was becoming less about proving what had always been inside him and more about recording what he chose now.\n\nThat was better evidence anyway.\n","word_count":1742}},"after-always":{"after-always-34":{"number":34,"title":"The Potion He Cannot Brew","content":"There are potions for sleep.\n\nPotions for pain.\n\nPotions for fever, memory, luck, love's imitation, truth, strength, weakness, and the temporary rearrangement of nearly every inconvenient thing a human body can do.\n\nThere is no potion for regret.\n\nSnape hated this fact.\n\nI discovered that when he nearly poisoned himself trying to create one.\n\nNot intentionally.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\nThe accident began with a commission from St Mungo's for a draught to reduce intrusive memory after curse trauma. Nothing that erased memory. Nothing that suppressed judgment. A therapeutic potion meant to take the sharpest edge off involuntary recollection so patients could sleep.\n\nSnape became obsessed.\n\nAt first, productively.\n\nHe researched old Calming Draughts, Dreamless Sleep, and experimental treatments from the first war. He rejected anything addictive, anything that dulled cognition too heavily, anything that interfered with Occlumency.\n\nThen he stopped sleeping.\n\nI noticed on day three.\n\nHe denied it.\n\nI noticed on day four.\n\nHe called me intrusive.\n\nOn day five, I found him in the laboratory at four in the morning with three cauldrons running and his left hand shaking badly enough to spill powdered moonstone.\n\nI turned off the nearest flame.\n\nHe swore.\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\n“Turn it back on.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The timing—”\n\n“Is ruined. Good.”\n\nHis face went white with anger.\n\n“That batch required six hours.”\n\n“You require sleep.”\n\n“I require results.”\n\n“For St Mungo's?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Only?”\n\nSilence.\n\nThere it was.\n\nI looked at the three cauldrons.\n\n“You're trying to brew it for yourself.”\n\nHis expression closed.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Severus.”\n\nHe flinched at the name.\n\nNot because he disliked it.\n\nBecause I used it when I needed him present.\n\n“You want something that will make the memories stop hurting.”\n\n“I want a functional treatment.”\n\n“For yourself.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Among others.”\n\nI softened.\n\n“That isn't wrong.”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\n“I didn't say it was.”\n\n“You're acting like wanting relief is a moral failure.”\n\nHis hand reached for the ingredient tray.\n\nI moved it away.\n\n“Enough.”\n\n“Do not touch my work.”\n\n“Then stop using your work to hurt yourself.”\n\nHis eyes went cold.\n\n“I am not hurting myself.”\n\n“You are exhausted, shaking, and brewing three experimental neuroactive potions alone.”\n\n“I know what I am doing.”\n\n“So did you at sixteen when you invented Sectumsempra.”\n\nThe words hit like a slap.\n\nSnape recoiled.\n\nI regretted them instantly.\n\nNot the concern.\n\nThe weapon.\n\nI had used his past to win.\n\nExactly what he had once done to me.\n\n“I'm sorry,” I said immediately.\n\nHis face was blank.\n\n“That was cruel.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I was scared and I reached for something that would hurt enough to stop you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I hate that I did it.”\n\nHe looked away.\n\nThe nearest cauldron bubbled.\n\nI extinguished it too.\n\nThis time he did not stop me.\n\n“I am still asking you to stop,” I said.\n\nHis shoulders lowered.\n\n“Fine.”\n\nNot agreement.\n\nSurrender from exhaustion.\n\nI took the stirring rod from his hand.\n\nHis fingers were cold.\n\n“Sit.”\n\n“You are enjoying this.”\n\n“Immensely.”\n\nHe sat.\n\nI brought water.\n\nHe drank because he was too tired to argue well.\n\nThen his face changed.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\nSnape looked at the cauldron behind me.\n\nThe experimental mixture had turned from blue to clear.\n\n“That should not happen.”\n\nI turned.\n\nA thin vapor curled upward.\n\nSweet.\n\nToo sweet.\n\n“Don't breathe—”\n\nToo late.\n\nThe room tilted.\n\nSnape moved faster than I had seen him move in months.\n\nHe shoved me toward the door and cast a shield over the cauldrons.\n\nMy knees failed.\n\nHe caught me.\n\nThe irony would have been funny under different circumstances.\n\n“Stay awake.”\n\nHis voice came from far away.\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“Concentrated dream suppressant. The stabilizer inverted.”\n\n“That sounds bad.”\n\n“It is bad.”\n\n“Excellent.”\n\nMy mouth felt numb.\n\nSnape half-carried me into the corridor.\n\n“Help!” he shouted.\n\nI had never heard him shout for help.\n\nThe sound scared me more than the potion.\n\nMadam Pomfrey arrived with McGonagall minutes later.\n\nI remember pieces.\n\nA bitter antidote.\n\nSnape's hand gripping mine.\n\nPomfrey ordering him out.\n\nSnape refusing.\n\nMy own voice saying his name because I could not think of anything else.\n\nThen sleep.\n\nWhen I woke in the hospital wing, afternoon light filled the windows.\n\nSnape sat beside the bed.\n\nHe looked terrible.\n\n“You look terrible,” I said.\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“Good. You are still tactless.”\n\n“You're welcome.”\n\nHe took my hand.\n\nHis grip was too tight.\n\n“I'm okay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Any damage?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You?”\n\n“I was shielded by position.”\n\n“Convenient.”\n\nHis mouth did not move.\n\nI realized he had been crying.\n\nNot currently.\n\nBut recently enough that the skin beneath his eyes was red.\n\nMy chest hurt.\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Do not comfort me for nearly killing you.”\n\n“You didn't.”\n\n“My potion did.”\n\n“An experimental accident—”\n\n“Caused by exhaustion I refused to acknowledge.”\n\nHis voice broke slightly.\n\nI did not argue.\n\nHe needed responsibility, not denial.\n\n“Yes,” I said.\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“You were reckless.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You scared me.”\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I am angry.”\n\n“You should be.”\n\n“And I love you.”\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“That is not absolution.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“It is not permission to do it again.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Now you can comfort me.”\n\nHis expression crumpled by a fraction.\n\nHe lifted my fingers to his mouth and kissed them.\n\n“I am sorry,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\nPomfrey returned before visiting hours ended. She did not ask whether we had forgiven one another.\n\nShe carried a form.\n\n\"This is the incident report,\" she said. \"I expect an accurate account, not an impressive one.\"\n\nSeverus looked offended.\n\n\"Madam Pomfrey—\"\n\n\"You may keep that expression. You may not omit the fact that you were working while exhausted.\"\n\nHe went quiet.\n\nI asked whether I should write a statement as well.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Not because I need you to assign blame, but because you breathed the vapor. The treatment team needs to know what you felt before your memory becomes unreliable.\"\n\nI described the sweet smell, the sudden weakness in my knees, and the way the sound of Severus's voice had seemed to move farther away. She wrote it down without asking me to dramatize it.\n\nSeverus watched her pen.\n\n\"You won't send Jasper's statement to the archive?\"\n\n\"No. It's a medical record. St Mungo's will receive what it needs to assess the research, and no more.\"\n\nI saw him relax a fraction.\n\nWhen she left, he reached for the report and then stopped.\n\n\"May I read your statement?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nHe stared.\n\n\"It's not a punishment,\" I said. \"I want to think about how much of it I'm comfortable sharing with you. I can still tell you how frightened I was.\"\n\nHe set the paper down.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHis voice was stiff, but he left the page where it was.\n\nI thought about our shared life: the people we loved, the separate commitments I had in Lanternweald, the boundaries we'd learned to name without making them evidence of rejection. This was another version of the same lesson. I could love him without giving up every private detail of my experience. He could be responsible for the accident without owning my account of it.\n\n\"Would you like me to stay?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Just don't tell me the symptoms again. I've already had the lecture.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"Your symptoms were extraordinarily inconsiderate.\"\n\n\"Apparently I've learned from you.\"\n\nHe pulled his chair nearer. We sat in silence until the last light left the windows.\n\nLater, Pomfrey banned both of us from the laboratory for three days.\n\nSnape objected.\n\nI did not.\n\nHe looked betrayed.\n\nThe potion project went on hold for a month.\n\nDuring the suspension, a St Mungo's researcher asked for the complete formula and a list of potential trial volunteers. Severus read the letter twice at the kitchen table.\n\n\"They haven't examined the incident report.\"\n\n\"How can you tell?\"\n\n\"They refer to the stabilizer as harmless. A charming description, considering what it nearly did to you.\"\n\nHe began drafting a reply in handwriting sharp enough to draw blood. Pomfrey arrived to check on my breathing and found him working.\n\n\"You're refusing the trial?\" she asked.\n\n\"I'm refusing an unsupervised trial based on a faulty account of the risk. There is a distinction.\"\n\n\"Excellent. Put that in your first sentence instead of your fourth paragraph.\"\n\n\"Madam Pomfrey, I am capable of writing a letter.\"\n\n\"I know. Most of them are very good at punishing the reader.\"\n\nI laughed and had to stop to catch my breath. Severus pushed my water glass toward me without looking up.\n\nAfter she left, I found the original draft torn in half.\n\n\"She was right,\" he said, before I could speak.\n\nIt seemed to cost him several teeth to admit it. The new letter requested independent review, an accurate adverse-effect record and specific patient consent. He did not offer his own exhaustion as evidence that anybody should risk a trial.\n\nAfter the owl left, he watched the window for a long time. I couldn't tell whether he was relieved or furious that competent caution was taking longer than brilliance.\n\nWhen we returned to it, we worked only in daylight, only one experimental cauldron at a time, with a third reviewer from St Mungo's present.\n\nThe final formula did not erase regret.\n\nIt did not erase nightmares.\n\nIt simply made sleep easier for some patients without stealing the memories that belonged to them.\n\nSnape called the result limited.\n\nI called it humane.\n\nHe eventually agreed.\n\nThere are potions for many things.\n\nThere are no potions that make the past stop being true.\n\nMaybe that was mercy too.","word_count":1603}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-35":{"number":35,"title":"The Question No One Owes","content":"The question came from a reporter who had learned nothing from experience.\n\nWe were leaving the opening ceremony for the rebuilt eastern bridge when she pushed through the crowd and shouted, “Avatar Aang! Which of your partners will bear the future Air Nation?”\n\nThe world stopped.\n\nNot literally.\n\nIf it had, Toph would have found a way to blame Aang.\n\nBut inside me, something froze.\n\nAang turned.\n\nKatara’s face went cold.\n\nToph’s foot struck the stone once.\n\nThe reporter, apparently committed to her own destruction, continued.\n\n“Is there an agreement about succession? The public has a legitimate interest in—”\n\n“No,” Aang said.\n\nOne word.\n\nNot loud.\n\nAbsolute.\n\nThe reporter blinked.\n\nAang stepped toward her.\n\n“The public has no legitimate interest in anyone’s reproductive choices.”\n\nCameras did not exist in our world, thank the spirits, but I could feel every sketch artist in the crowd committing the moment to history.\n\nThe reporter tried again.\n\n“As the last Air Nomad—”\n\n“I am the last surviving Air Nomad,” Aang said. “I am not the owner of every future Air Nomad.”\n\nKatara touched his arm.\n\nNot to stop him.\n\nTo stand there.\n\nThe reporter looked toward her.\n\n“Master Katara, surely you have discussed—”\n\nKatara smiled.\n\nIt was the smile that had destroyed Wen Haru in a hearing room.\n\n“We have discussed boundaries.”\n\nThe reporter swallowed.\n\nToph leaned toward me.\n\n“I can bury her shoes.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Just the shoes.”\n\n“No.”\n\nAang ended the questions and we left.\n\nThe carriage ride home was silent.\n\nThe public statement would be easy.\n\nWe had already made the principle clear after the donor meeting.\n\nThe private conversation was not easy.\n\nBecause the reporter had dragged a question into the open that we had intentionally left unfinished.\n\nChildren.\n\nNot Air Nation duty.\n\nOurs.\n\nAt home, nobody went to separate rooms.\n\nWe ended up at the kitchen table.\n\nOf course.\n\nToph poured tea badly on purpose and shoved cups toward everyone.\n\nKatara wrapped both hands around hers.\n\nAang stared at the steam.\n\nI said, “We don’t have to talk about it because somebody else asked.”\n\nAang looked up.\n\n“Do you want to talk about it?”\n\nI hated the answer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nToph groaned. “Fine. But nobody makes a chart.”\n\nI slowly put down my pen.\n\n“Oppression.”\n\nKatara almost laughed.\n\nAang took a breath.\n\n“I want children someday.”\n\nWe knew that.\n\nHearing it inside this conversation made it new.\n\nHe continued. “I don’t know how many. I don’t know what that family looks like. I don’t need biological children from any specific person.”\n\nToph said, “Good opening.”\n\nAang gave her a look.\n\nKatara spoke next.\n\n“I think I want children.”\n\nAang turned toward her.\n\nKatara held up one hand.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“I said think.”\n\nHis face softened.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nKatara looked down at her tea.\n\n“When I was younger, I assumed I would. Everyone did. Then the war ended and people started talking about Aang’s future children like they already existed.”\n\nHer jaw tightened.\n\n“I hated it enough that sometimes I couldn’t tell whether I didn’t want children or just didn’t want strangers wanting them for me.”\n\nAang’s face hurt to look at.\n\nHe did not interrupt.\n\nToph tapped one finger against the table.\n\n“My turn?”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\nToph leaned back.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nSimple.\n\n“Kids are loud. Pregnancy sounds horrifying. People already treat my body like public property whenever I’m injured, so that part sounds worse.”\n\nNo one corrected the assumption embedded in her specific fear. It was hers to name.\n\nToph continued. “I like teaching. I like some kids. I don’t want anybody hearing that and deciding it means I secretly want to be a mother.”\n\n“Understood,” I said.\n\nShe pointed at me. “Your turn, Paperwork.”\n\nMy pulse jumped.\n\nThe part of me that resisted fixed answers wanted to dodge the question.\n\nThe person living this life needed one honest enough for now.\n\n“I don’t know either.”\n\nNobody moved.\n\n“I know I don’t want my body treated as an answer to Aang’s culture.”\n\nAang’s hand tightened around his cup.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThis time he caught himself.\n\n“I understand.”\n\nI smiled faintly.\n\n“I know I could love a child. That’s not the same as wanting to raise one.”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n“I know family can happen in more ways than birth.”\n\nAang looked at me.\n\n“Adoption?”\n\n“Maybe. Fostering. Nieces and nephews who live in our kitchen. Students who become family. Children one of us has. Children none of us has.”\n\nToph said, “A very loud collection of strays.”\n\n“You say that like we aren’t already one.”\n\nShe grinned.\n\nThe pressure eased slightly.\n\nThen Katara asked the hardest question.\n\n“What if one of us wants children and somebody else never does?”\n\nSilence.\n\nThere was no answer that turned love into compatibility by force.\n\nAang stared at the table.\n\n“We would have to decide what that means.”\n\nToph frowned.\n\n“That’s terrible.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You’re supposed to have a wise Avatar answer.”\n\n“I don’t.”\n\n“Get one.”\n\nAang laughed once.\n\nThen his expression turned serious again.\n\n“I don’t want staying together to require anybody to become a parent.”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n“Neither do I.”\n\nToph said, “Obviously.”\n\nI looked at the three of them.\n\n“And I don’t want staying together to require somebody to give up being a parent if that becomes something they truly need.”\n\nThat hurt too.\n\nBecause it made room for futures where love was not enough to keep every shape intact.\n\nWe sat with it.\n\nNo promises we could not know how to keep.\n\nAang reached across the table.\n\n“Can we agree on now?”\n\n“What about now?” Katara asked.\n\n“No children now.”\n\nToph raised her hand immediately. “Strong support.”\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nI nodded.\n\nAang did too.\n\n“Then we revisit it,” he said. “Not because the public asks. Because we do.”\n\n“Yearly?” I asked.\n\nToph pointed at me. “Chart behavior.”\n\n“Seasonally?”\n\n“Worse.”\n\nKatara said, “When it feels necessary.”\n\nToph nodded. “Human answer.”\n\nI sighed dramatically and put the pen away.\n\nThat night, the newspaper printed the reporter’s question in enormous type.\n\nThe answer was smaller.\n\nThat seemed typical.\n\nI folded the paper and used it to line the bottom of a kitchen bin.\n\nAang watched me.\n\n“Symbolic?”\n\n“Practical.”\n\nToph called from the other room, “Same thing if you do it right!”\n\nI smiled.\n\nThe future remained unwritten.\n\nThat was not a failure of planning.\n\nNo one was owed certainty about our children.\n\nNot donors.\n\nNot reporters.\n\nNot history.\n\nNot even the versions of ourselves sitting around the table tonight.\n\nWe owed one another honesty as the answer changed.\n\nNothing more could be promised without turning possibility into duty.\n\nThree days later, the question came back disguised as an invitation.\n\nA community school asked Aang to teach an Air Nomad lesson. Tucked under the invitation was a note from the same reporter, asking whether he could announce plans for future airbenders while the children watched.\n\nAang stared at it. \"I was going to show them how to make a glider.\"\n\n\"Then show them,\" Katara said, sorting bandages for the clinic. \"The teacher invited you to teach. Not to put our family on display.\"\n\n\"What if the students ask about the future?\"\n\n\"Answer what you know,\" I said. \"You know how to teach them.\"\n\nToph flicked the paper with one finger. \"Tell the reporter she can see what happens to a paper plane in a mud pit.\"\n\n\"Toph,\" Katara warned.\n\n\"What? It's educational.\"\n\nAt the school, the first glider nosedived into a vegetable patch. Aang apologized to the gardener, who informed him that if the Avatar could fly, he could replant cabbages. He did. The students laughed at the actual lesson: a good teacher can make a mistake.\n\nKatara helped one pupil whose wrist hurt and asked whether they'd prefer watching instead. Toph took two others to a rough threshold and made them feel the slope through their shoes. When they wanted to smooth the entire path, she made them find the one stone that really needed moving.\n\nNobody was being taught the same thing.\n\nAfterward, the teacher put the attendance list away where the reporter couldn't copy it. Aang came home with dirt on his collar and a clearer idea for his public response: schools, teachers, living traditions and choice, not an imaginary family's biological schedule.\n\nToph sniffed his sleeve. \"Cabbage.\"\n\n\"It was a learning experience,\" he said.\n\n\"Smells like one.\"\n\nKatara kissed the top of his head. He looked ridiculous and pleased.\n\nA week later, Aang addressed the question publicly without mentioning any of our private choices.\n\nHe spoke about culture, teaching, community, recovered history, and the fact that a people could not be reduced to bloodline arithmetic.\n\nHe did not promise future children.\n\nHe did not volunteer any of us as evidence.\n\nWhen he came home, Toph said, “Not terrible.”\n\nKatara kissed him.\n\nI handed him tea.\n\nHe looked more relieved by our ordinary reactions than he had by the applause outside.\n\nAfterward, Aang asked whether his answer had been enough.\n\n“It was yours,” I said. “And it left ours private.”\n\nKatara nodded.\n\nToph added, “Also you didn’t say anything stupid about destiny.”\n\nAang looked wounded. “I like destiny.”\n\n“We know,” three of us said.\n\nThe future felt lighter once nobody had to earn it with a body.\n\nNobody at our table disagreed.\n\nFor that night, that answer was enough.\n","word_count":1545}}};for(const series of lib.series){const updates=changes[series.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of series.chapters){const e=updates[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}})();
